WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.240
- We tend to think of
laws as restrictions,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.960
things that you can't do or
things that you're forced

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.010
to do against your will,
clean up your room,

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.680
stand up straight, pick up
your feet, take it like a man,

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.530
be nice to your sister,
don't mix beer and wine ever.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:13.370
And oh yeah.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.330
- Don't drive on the railroad track.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:18.830
- Oh, Phil, that's one
I happen to agree with.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:21.140
- But did you know that sometimes
the law can actually lift

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.970
these restrictions and give you freedoms

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.850
to do things you wouldn't
otherwise be able to do.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.980
Yes, sometimes the law can give you rights

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.430
that you don't even know about.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.800
Not that you'd necessarily want
to use some of these rights,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:33.900
but still, let's talk about some

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.620
of those rights that
you didn't know you had.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.660
(playful upbeat music)

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.160
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.880
Now warning, restrictions may apply.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.310
Please see your doctor before attempting

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.840
any of these freedoms,
and more importantly,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.860
please consult with your attorney.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.870
So let's kick things off with
a woman's right to go topless.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.390
In 2015, the Fort Collins
City Council enacted

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:54.920
a public nudity ordinance,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.380
banning women from removing
their shirts in public.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:58.820
I'm not gonna read the whole statute,

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.020
you can do that yourselves,

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.660
but obviously the law bans women

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.220
from doing something men
are legally allowed to do.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.330
A women's group sued the city,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:08.570
arguing that a female-only
topless ban violated

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:09.830
the Equal Protection Clause.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.500
How did the city defend the band?

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.390
Well, for starters, it argued that men

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.330
and women's chests were totally different,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.667
which also means women, quote,

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:19.800
"Have a hallowed sexual
status in Western culture."

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.240
The city tried hard to make it sound

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.270
as if this reasoning was based

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.010
on something other than the stereotypes,

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:26.740
but this did not go
over particularly well.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.270
For a gender-based classification

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.060
to survive a legal challenge,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.857
the government needs to show, quote,

00:01:31.857 --> 00:01:34.260
"An exceedingly persuasive justification",

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.880
that the classification is necessary.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.407
That classification must serve, quote,

00:01:38.407 --> 00:01:40.450
"an important governmental objective",

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:41.283
through means, quote,

00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:44.380
"Substantially related to
achieving those objectives."

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.230
What objectives did the ban serve?

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:49.270
Well, Fort Colin said it
served two main purposes.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.450
First, the city's ban
would protect children

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.040
from seeing a female breast,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.670
because obviously that's not
the defining characteristic

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.070
of a mammal and mammalian children

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.710
never see a female breast
in their entire lives.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.980
And second, it would
prevent general lawlessness,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.190
especially traffic problems that may occur

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.570
when people see a topless woman in public.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.630
Ah, yes, men can't control themselves,

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.500
so let's limit what women can do.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.640
The court made quick work
of the city's reasoning.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.670
Although other courts have
decided this differently,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.420
the 10th Circuit thought this was bunk

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.360
and that women's rights
were being violated.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.220
Fort Collins spent $300,000 on this case

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.850
and they decided to throw in the towel

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.610
rather than appealing
it to the Supreme Court.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.810
So if you live in Utah, Colorado, Wyoming,

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.560
New Mexico, Kansas, and Oklahoma,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.290
you can take your shirt off
outside, no matter who you are.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.040
The right to get married by proxy.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.610
Most people are familiar with the process

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:36.560
for getting hitched.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.540
Get a marriage license, have a ceremony,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.330
file the completed license with the state,

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.060
discover irreconcilable differences,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.670
hire a lawyer, exact your revenge,

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:46.006
wish you were dead, get
divorced, and repeat.

00:02:46.006 --> 00:02:47.120
Ha (laughs)

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:48.910
But did he know it's
possible to get married

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.720
without showing up for the wedding itself?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.450
Well, if you're overseas in the military

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.620
or just have something
better to do that day,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.800
someone else can actually stand

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.200
in for you in many different states.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.050
This is known as a proxy marriage
because someone stands in

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.210
for the other party to take the vows.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.200
To do this, you need
to authorize your agent

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.810
to get married on your behalf.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.770
Now, most states allow a proxy marriage,

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.420
where one person can't be present,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.680
but in Montana a wedding can take place

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.730
without either party being present.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.000
This is what's called a
double proxy marriage.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.010
Now to qualify for a double
proxy wedding in Montana,

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.040
at least one party must be on active duty

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.160
in the military or a resident of Montana

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.540
at the time of the
application for the license.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.780
Why would someone want
to do this, you ask?

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.130
Other than the obvious reason

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.900
that you actually love this person?

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.980
Well cold, hard cash, obviously.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:33.990
For service members,

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.050
marriage can be a great
financial arrangement.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:37.390
The army will actually increase your pay

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.510
by at least $1,200 a
month once you're married.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.500
Now, the Montana Marriage Law
was first passed in the 1860s,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.470
when men poured into the
area to work in the mines.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.120
The miners didn't have
the time or the money

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.920
to travel home to get their fiances,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.280
so the state passed a
law allowing ceremonies

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.460
to take place without
either person being present.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.840
Proxy marriage isn't just
for the military though,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.430
some states allow prisoners to
get married through a proxy.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:02.700
In 1987, the Supreme Court
case, known as Turner vs Safley,

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.170
granted prisoners the
constitutional right to marry.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.270
However, the right to
marry is also subject

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:09.030
to reasonable prison regulations,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.677
as long as the regulations are, quote,

00:04:10.677 --> 00:04:13.960
"Reasonably related to legitimate
penological interests."

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:15.936
Get your mind out of the gutter.

00:04:15.936 --> 00:04:18.760
- Ah, come on, we're all just
trying to have a good time.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.870
- This means prisons can
do all sorts of things

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:22.920
to delay or prevent marriages.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.790
In 2013, Texas simply
outlawed proxy marriages,

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.200
including for inmates.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.340
Their main concern was
that people were marrying

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.810
just to convey benefits.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.780
And that kind of fraudulent
arrangement was featured

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:34.990
on the popular reality
show, "Love After Lockup",

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:37.940
where a doofus named Vince
was going to marry ex-inmate,

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.980
Amber, and adopt her girlfriend, Puppy,

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.400
as some sort of insurance
and pension scheme.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.060
- I'm a bold mother (beep).

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.970
- Though three years later
the state reversed itself

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.800
after advocates pointed
out that the law was

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.570
in direct conflict with Turner vs Safley.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.940
The right to an annulment if
you get married on a dare.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.060
So you have the right to get married

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.630
without being present at
the ceremony, fantastic,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.890
but what if your best
friend double dog dares you

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:01.940
to get married to that
hose beast over there,

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.490
and like Marty McFly, you
follow through on the dare,

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.730
then you sober up, look at your
bride or groom next to you,

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:09.410
and say, "Oh no, I got married
because someone dared me to"?

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.303
You know, that old chestnut.

00:05:13.305 --> 00:05:14.650
(bell chiming)

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.420
- Got it a week after we started dating.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.370
- Well relax, because if you
live in Delaware or Colorado,

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:22.220
getting married on a dare
is grounds for an annulment.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:24.020
And unlike divorce, an annulment treats

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.000
the marriage as if it never existed.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.660
Annulments are usually only
available if there was fraud,

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.560
or coercion, or illegality
at the time of the marriage,

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.370
but Delaware and Colorado include a,

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:37.070
got married as a sort of joke,
as a ground for annulment.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.120
The relevant law in Delaware

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.230
is 13 Delaware Code Section 1506,

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:43.807
which includes this as a
justification for annulment,

00:05:43.807 --> 00:05:45.460
"One or both parties entered into

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.250
the marriage as a jest or dare."

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:48.510
Now of course the state requires

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.240
some proof of the jest or dare,

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.600
and the petition for divorce
or annulment form asks you

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:56.220
to describe how and when you
learned of the jest or dare.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.270
So if you are married for
decades with several children,

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.990
don't expect the court to
just rubber stamp your claim

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.650
that you got married on a dare
and it should be nullified.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:06.960
The right to get away with murder

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.620
in Yellowstone National Park.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:11.470
Now, speaking of marriage,
let's talk about murder.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.310
Have you ever (laughs) (beep).

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.140
Have you ever fantasized about
committing the perfect crime?

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.530
Well, Michigan State law professor,

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:18.950
Brian Kalt, certainly has.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:20.410
He wrote a law review article explaining

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:21.800
how you could get away with murder

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:23.410
in Yellowstone National Park.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:24.720
Kalt grounds his argument

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.690
in the plain meaning of the constitution.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.260
Although Yellowstone is
mostly located in Wyoming,

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:32.360
about 9% of the park is
actually located in Montana,

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.670
and an even smaller portion,
about 50 square miles,

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:35.870
is located in Idaho.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.570
Yellowstone was established in 1872,

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.870
long before these states
were added to the Union.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:42.020
And when the states were admitted,

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:43.770
each seated over exclusive jurisdiction

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.950
of its portion of Yellowstone
to the federal government.

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:48.790
And both Article I, Section
8 and federal law give

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:50.700
the Federal Government
exclusive jurisdiction

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.370
over Yellowstone National Park

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.200
and state law doesn't apply there.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:55.860
Later, when Congress created

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.520
the Federal District Court for Wyoming,

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.380
it placed the entire park
under that jurisdiction.

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:03.150
That means that things that
happen in Montana and Idaho,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.170
on park lands,

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.420
are within the Wyoming
District Court's jurisdiction.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.030
However, Article III, Section
II of the Constitution

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.070
requires that the "Trial
be held in the state

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:13.130
in which the crime was committed."

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:15.590
This sets up the legal basis
for getting away with murder

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:18.870
if you commit a crime in
Yellowstone National Park in Idaho.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.037
And moreover, the 6th
Amendment requires the,

00:07:21.037 --> 00:07:23.000
"Impartial jury of the state and district

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.410
wherein the crime shall
have been committed."

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.990
So theoretically, the only
people available for the jury

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:31.530
must live in the Idaho section
of Yellowstone National Park.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.610
The thing is, no one lives
on the 50 square miles

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.990
of federal land that make
up this zone in Idaho.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.300
So you can see how small
that zone is on the map.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.590
And since no humans lived there,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.710
only these Idaho otters can judge you.

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:45.020
And this is why that
area has been nicknamed,

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.250
the zone of death.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.010
The Wyoming Federal
Court has jurisdiction,

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:49.610
but if you want to go to trial,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.970
the Wyoming Court simply
doesn't have jurisdiction.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.860
And although Kalt tried to get Congress

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.540
to fix this loophole, they
haven't done anything,

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:59.080
shocker, and that loophole still exists.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.070
So if you want to visit the Idaho portion

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:02.970
of Yellowstone National Park,

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:04.660
Idaho is just dying to meet you.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:05.493
- Ka-kaa.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:08.770
- The right to run for Governor of Kansas

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.110
as a teen, dog, or
resident of another state.

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:13.330
In Kansas, almost anyone can be governor.

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:14.870
In Kansas, you can run for governor

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.470
before you're even eligible to vote.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.460
The state has absolutely no qualifications

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:19.840
for governatorial candidates.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:23.560
In 2018, seven teenagers, all
boys, ran for Kansas Governor.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:24.720
The Director of Elections

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.807
at the Kansas Secretary
State's Office said,

00:08:26.807 --> 00:08:28.340
"There's seriously nothing on the books

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:31.580
that lays out anything, no age,
no residency, no experience.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:32.413
Nothing."

00:08:32.413 --> 00:08:34.180
This was confirmed by Ballotpedia,

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:36.210
the website which tracks
all things related

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:37.920
to state and federal offices.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.470
Ballotpedia concludes
that, "Kansas' Constitution

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.560
does not set out requirements for office."

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.310
Cool, that means that even a
baby could run for governor.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:47.430
Someone alert Alec Baldwin
about a possible extension

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:49.190
of the "Boss Baby" cinematic universe.

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:52.970
- I may look like a baby,
but I was born all grown up.

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:54.270
- This is amazing for many reasons,

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.710
including the fact former
Kansas Secretary of State,

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.720
Kris Kobach, has spent the
last five years stoking fears

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.070
about mass voter fraud

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:03.870
and championing extremely
restrictive voter ID.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.000
- If it's not on the
list, it doesn't exist.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:06.833
- In fact,

00:09:06.833 --> 00:09:09.220
Kobach briefly ran President
Trump's election commission,

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.120
which was supposed to prove

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:13.080
that President Trump actually
won the popular vote in 2016.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:14.700
The commission disbanded after he found

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.060
no evidence of voter fraud.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:18.330
Kobach wanted it to be harder to vote,

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:20.790
and he proposed a federal law
requiring people to have proof

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.740
of American citizenship when
they showed up at the polls.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.480
But during his time as Secretary of State,

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.140
he never made any recommendations about

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.780
the state's non-existent
requirements to run for governor.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:32.090
Kansas legislators immediately
started discussing ways

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:34.000
to clarify the law, but nothing stopped

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.380
the election with the
teens from moving forward.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.630
And there's also no
residency requirements,

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.610
so candidates from Vermont
and New York threw their hats

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:42.470
into the ring as well.

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:44.990
It's not even clear that
a person has to be human

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:46.770
to qualify for Kansas Governor.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:47.810
The Director of Elections said

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:50.150
that although no dog has
ever attempted to run,

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.670
there's no law specifically stating

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:53.450
that a candidate has to be human.

